  "body": "## What changed\r\n\r\nA STEP model import states the length unit that the model was read in. A STEP file that states no length unit is never read in a guessed unit.\r\n\r\n- **Kernel** (`crates/kernel/src/step_assumed_unit.rs`, `solidcore_step.rs`, `solidcore_step_import.rs`): `read_step_assuming` and `import_step_model_assuming` take the caller's millimetres per unit. For a file whose contexts state no length unit, the unit is written into each representation context before the read. The tolerance, the edge-gap cap and the chord are then in the same unit as the geometry. A file that states its own unit keeps it. A stated size outside 0.001..=1000 mm is refused by name.\r\n- **Geometry** (`crates/geometry/src/step.rs`): `parse_open_assuming`. A file with no length unit and no caller unit is `StepError::MissingUnits`. `StepMesh` carries `length_units_mm` and `assumed_mm`.\r\n- **App**:\r\n  - `ImportError::StepUnitsMissing`.\r\n  - `ImportModel::within_in` and `from_bytes_in`, and the unit option on each import path: the file, the bytes, the sourced load and the cadmpeg conversion.\r\n  - A unit for a file that is not STEP is refused by name before parsing, as a facet budget is.\r\n  - The last import note names the unit: `length unit: in (stated by the file)`, `length unit: in (stated at import; the file states no length unit)`, or `... (stated by the file; the in stated at import is not used)`.\r\n- **MCP and script**: `import_model` takes `units` (`\"mm\"` or `\"in\"`). `cam.import` takes it as the fourth argument. `docs/agents/mcp-tools.md` and `assets/scripts/cam.d.ts` are regenerated.\r\n- **UI**: a refused unitless import opens the **Length unit** modal (`egui_app/step_units_prompt.rs`), with the buttons Millimeters (mm), Inches (in) and Cancel. The answer goes through the command queue (`CommandKind::ResolveStepUnits`) and runs the same import again. The import that lands is one undoable command. Cancel and Escape import nothing. An import-as-fixture binding is kept for the second import.\r\n- **Docs**: `units-and-coordinates.md` (it said that a unitless STEP file \"is kept as millimetres\") and a new `troubleshooting.md` entry. The decision record is `docs/decisions/1874-step-length-units.md`.\r\n\r\n## Screenshot\r\n\r\nThe Length unit prompt for a STEP file that states no length unit (the status line under it says the same):\r\n\r\n<img width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" alt=\"step-units-prompt\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/22e70cee-e23e-4f9a-b8db-53e4c28add37\" />\r\n\r\n\r\n## Decisions (see the decision record)\r\n\r\n- The file's own unit wins over the caller's (the DXF import does the reverse, #1862). A STEP unit context is an exact statement of the CAD system.\r\n- The chosen unit is not recorded on the model. A linked-file re-import asks again.\r\n- Tool holders, fixtures and tool bodies from a unitless STEP file are refused by name, with no prompt.\r\n\r\n## Pinning tests\r\n\r\n- `camcraft-kernel` `step_assumed_unit` (3): a unitless box reads 25.4² times the area in inches; the file's own unit wins; an out-of-range unit is refused by name.\r\n- `camcraft-app` `step_units_import` (5): the typed refusal; the inch and mm imports and their notes; the file's mm, in and m notes; a unit for an STL is refused; MCP `import_model` with no unit, a bad unit and `in`.\r\n- `camcraft-ui` lib `step_units_tests` (5): the question, the answer as one undoable import (inch is 25.4 times the mm width), Cancel, and no question for a file that states its unit.\r\n- `camcraft-ui` lib `egui_app::tests::step_units_prompt` (4): the labels, no prompt without a question, a unit button, Cancel and Escape.\r\n- Updated, because each STEP import now ends with the unit note: `import_step_outward` (3 tests) and `import_step_closed_shell` `a_flipped_face_is_named_in_the_import_result_and_the_mcp_answer`. They assert the unit note in place of \"no notes\". No assertion is weaker.\r\n\r\n## Gates\r\n\r\nFinal head b5637db2b (the review fixes on 2649bb17e), Windows x86_64, without sccache:\r\n\r\n- Quick gate (`cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/main`): 1033 steps, 1032 ok. The one red step is `ai_guide_calls_match_live_tools_list`, the CRLF defect #3119, which this PR does not touch.\r\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-kernel -p camcraft-geometry -p camcraft-app -p camcraft-ui --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean (kernel and geometry on 2649bb17e; they did not change after it).\r\n- Heavy lanes (`cargo xtask gate heavy --base upstream/main`): `kernel heavy` (17 steps), `motion heavy` (37 steps) and `app heavy` (198 steps), after one release build. 253 steps ok, 0 failed, 26.1 min. The known-fail steps of #1950 and #2849 are red as expected. (The run on 2649bb17e also had 253 steps ok.)\r\n- Note: with `RUSTC_WRAPPER=sccache`, two gate runs here failed with \"can't find crate\" (`num_enum`, then `flecs_ecs`) and the warning `ignoring -C extra-filename flag due to -o flag`. The runs without sccache are correct. This is a local cache matter, not this change.\r\n\r\n## Review fixes (b5637db2b)\r\n\r\n- `crates/app/tests/cadmpeg_import.rs`: the eight `load_open_within` calls in the `#[cfg(unix)]` module pass `None` for the new `units` argument. This module does not compile on Windows, so it is not in the Windows gates above. It needs a build on Linux or macOS.\r\n- `State::take_step_units`: each import start takes the chosen unit, whatever its file. A unit never waits for a later import of the same file. Pinned by `step_units_tests::a_chosen_unit_never_waits_for_a_later_import_of_the_same_file`.\r\n- `troubleshooting.md`: the answer is not kept; the next import, a linked file's re-import included, asks again.\r\n\r\n## Not exercised\r\n\r\n- A unitless file from the cadmpeg converter was not tried. That path uses the same `parse_model_open_in`.\r\n- `crates/app/src/mcp/tools.rs` and `crates/app/src/script/api.rs` were already over 400 lines. They grow by 2 and 7 lines.\r\n\r\nChangelog: A STEP import states the model's length unit, and a STEP file that states no unit is no longer read as millimetres: the desktop app asks for mm or inches, and MCP and scripts take `units` (#1874)\r\n\r\nFixes #1874\r\n\r\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\r\n\r\n\r\n",
